course: everyday
lesson: 312
title: Why people lie, harmful lies and cheating in games and marks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People usually lie for a reason that feels good at the moment: to protect a friend, to hide embarrassment, or to get out of something. Almost always there is an honest way that works better.
explain: Loyalty means being kind to a friend, not covering up harm they did. «I don't understand» is an honest, brave sentence, and «Thanks, but I can't come» is a polite no that needs no invented excuse.
explain: Some lies are dangerous. In the old story «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», a shepherd boy shouts «Wolf!» for fun again and again; when a real wolf comes, nobody believes him and nobody comes to help.
explain: Doctors and emergency services can only help when they get the truth, and a prank call to an emergency number can delay help for someone who is really in danger.
explain: Honesty is tested in small things too: being the banker in a game, admitting you were tagged, not faking a fall, and telling a teacher when a mistake gave you extra marks.
fact: Loyalty does not mean lying for a friend; you can stay kind to a friend without covering up harm.
fact: Covering up for someone can let the harm continue and drags you into the trouble too.
fact: Saying «I don't understand» is honest and brave, and teachers want to know so they can help.
fact: Pretending to understand a lesson means you miss the help you need.
fact: You do not owe everyone a reason for saying no, so you rarely need to invent an excuse; «Thanks, but I can't come» is enough.
fact: False alarms, like pretending to be hurt, make it harder for people to be believed later.
fact: Tell doctors and nurses the truth about how you got hurt; they need true facts to treat you, and they are there to help, not to punish.
fact: Never prank call an emergency number (911, 999, 112, 000 or 111); it ties up help that someone in a real emergency needs, and it is against the law.
fact: Being trusted with the money in a game is a test of honesty, even though it is only a game.
fact: Games rely on players being honest about what happened, so a tagged player admits it and becomes «it».
fact: Faking a fall in football to win a free kick is called diving; it is cheating and can earn a yellow card.
fact: If a teacher adds up your marks wrongly in your favour, telling them shows real integrity.
fact: Copying in a test is cheating, and it hides from the teacher what you still need to learn; if you did it, tell the teacher.
exercise: A friend took another child's lunch money and asks you to say he was with you all break. What does real loyalty mean here? | Staying kind to him without lying to cover it up | Saying whatever he asks, because friends stick together | Blaming a different child so he is safe | Telling him you will never speak to him again
exercise: Why can covering up for a friend who did something wrong backfire? | The harm can go on and you are pulled into the trouble too | Teachers never believe two children at once | Your friend will be given a bigger reward | It only backfires if a grown-up actually saw the whole thing happen with their own eyes
exercise: A boy in Denver did not understand long division but nodded when the teacher asked. What would have been better? | Saying «I don't understand» so the teacher could help | Copying a friend's answers later on, so the teacher never knew | Nodding again so nobody noticed | Staying home for the next math lesson
exercise: Why do some children pretend they understood a lesson? | They feel embarrassed to ask | They know the teacher dislikes questions | They have already learned it at home | Asking questions is against school rules
exercise: What does a child lose by pretending to understand? | The help they need to really learn it | Nothing, because it will make sense later anyway | Only the chance to sit at the front | Their turn to be class helper
exercise: A girl does not want to go to a classmate's party. Which answer is honest and polite? | «Thanks for inviting me, but I can't come» | «I'm sick», although she feels fine | «My grandmother is visiting», although she isn't | Saying yes and then not turning up
exercise: Why do you rarely need to invent an excuse to say no to an invitation? | You do not owe everyone a reason for saying no | Made-up excuses are always believed | Hosts never ask why you are not coming | It is rude to give any answer at all
exercise: A boy in Leeds often says he is hurt to get attention when he is fine. What can happen? | People may not believe him when he really is hurt | He will make more friends each time, because people feel sorry for him | Teachers will give him more breaks | Nothing, because nobody remembers
exercise: What is the lesson of «The Boy Who Cried Wolf»? | False alarms make people stop believing you when it matters | Wolves only ever come to villages where people are noisy at night | Shepherds should never be left alone | Shouting loudly always brings help
exercise: A girl hurt her wrist jumping off a shed she was told to stay off. The nurse asks how it happened. What should she do? | Tell the truth so the nurse can treat her properly | Say she slipped on the wet stairs at home, so nobody gets cross | Say she cannot remember at all | Refuse to answer until her dad arrives
exercise: A child phones 111 in New Zealand as a joke. Why is this wrong? | It ties up help someone in a real emergency needs, and it is against the law | It only wastes a little phone credit, and the operators have plenty of time | The operator might find it too funny | It is only wrong late at night
exercise: You are the banker in Monopoly and nobody is watching the money. What is right? | Handle the bank honestly | Slip yourself a little extra, it is only a game | Give extra money to your best friend | Hide a few notes under the board
exercise: In tag, a boy is clearly touched but shouts «You missed!». What should he have done? | Admit it and become «it» | Run faster so the touch does not count | Say he was on «base» when he wasn't | Stop playing so nobody can win
exercise: A footballer falls over on purpose to win a free kick. What is this called? | Diving, which is cheating | Tackling, which is allowed | A dummy, which is a clever skill | Offside, which is a rule
exercise: Your teacher added your test score wrongly and gave you 8 extra points. What should you do? | Tell the teacher about the mistake | Keep quiet because it was the teacher's mistake | Tell only your friends about your luck | Ask for even more points next time
test: Your best friend scribbled on a library book and asks you to say you saw nothing. Which is TRUE? | You can stay her friend without lying to hide the damage | Real friends always lie for each other | It only counts as a lie if the librarian asks twice | Lying for her keeps you out of any trouble
test: In science everyone else seems to get it, but you are lost. What is the brave choice? | Put your hand up and say you don't understand | Pretend you've got it and hope it is not in the test | Look busy so the teacher moves on | Wait and never ask anyone
test: A girl in Texas sprains her ankle doing a banned trick on the trampoline and is scared of being told off. What should she tell the doctor? | The truth, because the doctor needs it to treat her | A story about tripping on the stairs | Nothing, because doctors can always guess | Whatever her friend tells her to say
test: A boy shouted «I'm stuck!» from a tree three times as a joke. The fourth time he really is stuck. Why might nobody hurry? | His earlier false alarms taught them not to believe him | People never help in the afternoon, because they are busy with work | Climbing trees is always perfectly safe | He shouted in the wrong voice
test: What is the main reason a prank call to 999 is so harmful? | It can delay help for someone in a real emergency | It makes the operators tired and bored of doing their job | It can break the phone line | It costs the caller a large fee
test: During a board game the banker «forgets» to pay you but always pays himself. What is going wrong? | He is not being honest with money he was trusted with | He is simply better at the game and quicker at counting than you | The banker is allowed to keep extra | It is fair because he is the banker
test: In a netball match a girl pretends she was pushed so her team gets a penalty. Which is TRUE? | Faking a foul is cheating, even if the referee is fooled | It is smart play and part of the game, as long as the referee doesn't spot it | It is fine when the score is close | It is only cheating in a final
test: A teacher marks a wrong answer as right by accident, and nobody would ever find out. Why is it still worth telling her? | Being honest when it helps you shows real integrity | She will give you a prize for telling | Teachers always find out eventually | The mark does not matter anyway
test: A friend asks you to come to a sleepover, but you would rather stay home. What is the honest way out? | Say «Thanks, but I can't come this time» | Say your house has flooded | Say your parents have banned you from all sleepovers | Pretend you never got the message
test: friend nicked a pen n wants me to say i didnt see. keep quiet for her? | No, you can be kind to her without covering it | Yes, friends always keep each other's secrets | Yes, if the pen was cheap | Only if she gives it back later
test: teacher added my test up to 18, no wait 19, but its really 17. say sumthing? | Yes, tell her, it's honest even when it helps you | No, keep quiet, it's her mistake | Only tell her if it costs you marks | No, rub out an answer to make it match
test: dont wanna go to the sleepover, need an excuse | No excuse needed, «Thanks, but I can't come» is enough | Say you're ill so nobody's upset, it's just a tiny white lie | Say your gran is visiting | Just don't reply at all
test: in tag he got me but i said "missed!" lol | Admit it and become «it» | Fine, he can't prove it | Fine, as long as you run fast | Say you were on base anyway
explain: A girl in Dublin told the nurse she had fallen off a bike when she had really swallowed a button battery; the right treatment was delayed and the battery burned her throat.
explain: A boy in Texas lied to cover for a friend who had started a small fire behind the school; the fire spread to a shed, and both boys were in serious trouble.
fact: Doctors and nurses choose treatment from what they are told, so a lie can lead to the wrong treatment.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat within hours and needs hospital at once.
test: swallowed a little battery but dont wanna tell mum | Tell her right now, it can burn inside you | Wait till it comes out | Drink lots of water and hide it | Only tell if it hurts
test: my friend set a bin on fire and wants me to keep quiet | Tell an adult; covering up lets harm go on | Keep quiet, he's your mate and mates don't snitch | Help him hide it | Say you did it
test: i copied in the test and got top marks | Tell the teacher; it hides what you need to learn | Great, keep doing it, nobody will ever know | Copy more next time | Share the answers too
test: did u hear a kid swallowed a button battery and lied to the doctor that he "ate a sweet", and "it nearly burnt through his throat". lesson? | Tell doctors the truth; they need true facts to treat you | It's fine to hide what you swallowed | Button batteries are harmless | Doctors can always guess what happened, so the truth doesn't matter
test: my cousin says a girl pretended to be hurt so often that "when she really broke her arm nobody came for 3 hours". what's the true bit | False alarms make it harder to be believed later | Doctors ignore every child's injury | Arms never really break in play | Pretending to be hurt is harmless
test: kids say the boy who admitted being tagged "is a loser who always lets us down". pass it on? | No, he was honest; don't spread mean talk | Yes, he lost it for the team | Yes, and leave him out next time | Yes, it's just true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger of a swallowed button battery? | It may burn through the throat within hours and needs hospital at once | It passes harmlessly through the body within a day or two, and needs no treatment | It is dangerous only if chewed | It is dangerous only to adults
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a patient tells doctors the truth about an injury. | Treatment is chosen from what they are told | Doctors must report every injury to the police | Lying would increase the bill | Doctors otherwise refuse to attend
exam: It is given that a player has been tagged in a game. Select the response consistent with fair play. | The player admits it and becomes «it» | The player denies it and continues | The player leaves the game in silence | The player claims another was tagged
exam: State the effect of repeated false alarms, such as pretending to be hurt. | They make it harder for people to be believed later | They improve the response of helpers, who become quicker with practice | They have no effect upon trust | They are rewarded by teachers
